450 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (The main goal of the Security Council for topic A is to reduce tensions between India and Pakistan, which took place in 1998 when both countries tested nuclear weapons and again in 1999 during the Kargil conflict. Their purpose is to stop new military actions along the Line of Control and prevent global nuclear war. Delegates should examine ways to accomplish this )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
451 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (Military Observer Group in India and Pakistan \(UNMOGIP\) in 1948. UNMOGIP was mandated to oversee the ceasefire in Kashmir after the first Indo-Pakistani war and monitor the ceasefire line for violations. While the mandate helped to maintain a ceasefire line, it could not settle the political dispute that resulted in the war. The delegates should consider whether a monitoring mechanism would help manage the disputes along the Line of Control. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
452 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (Finally, the US attempted to manage the Kargil conflict through diplomatic channels. On 4 July 1999, President Bill Clinton met Pakistani Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif in Washington. After the talks, Pakistan agreed to withdraw its troops from the Indian side of the Line of Control. The talks helped to end the immediate crisis, but they did not settle the Kashmir dispute or establish a permanent crisis-management system. The talks also raised the question of the relevance of the Security Council as a conflict-management organ because the involvement of one superpower seemed to overshadow the actions of the council. The delegates should consider how the Security Council can help de-escalate conflicts without the intervention of major powers. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
453 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (The most significant Security Council action before and during this crisis was the adoption of resolution 1172 by acclamation on 6 June 1998. After India and Pakistan conducted nuclear tests in May 1998, the council condemned the tests and demanded that both states halt all tests, stop developing nuclear-weapons delivery systems and ballistic missiles, and urge states not to provide technology that could contribute to the programs. The resolution marked a near-unanimous agreement that South Asia was not a suitable region for nuclear testing. However, the resolution did not impose sanctions, set up a monitoring mechanism, or a mediation process. Consequently, India and Pakistan did not become a party to the NPT or CTBT, and a Kargil conflict erupted within a year of the adoption of this resolution. Future proposals should consider implementing more effective verification measures, controlling exports, and encouraging the states to embrace arms-control treaties. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
