/T (Topic B: Rise of terrorist organizations in the Middle East and Africa in 1998 )
/E (Topic B: Rise of terrorist organizations in the Middle East and Africa in 1998 )
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
493 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 173 0 R
/E (The demise of the Soviet Union in 1991 led to a large shift within the international politics and security landscape. During the Cold War international security was focused on great )
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
494 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 173 0 R
/E (The security situation in the international community became more complex in 1998, as Terrorist organisations became more prominent. Since the fall of the Soviet Union in 1991 there have been many changes to the security situation in the international community. This has led to several non-state actors using armed conflict to challenge national governments and the international community. This has been most apparent in some countries in the middle east and Africa. Where there has been political unrest and armed conflict, there have also been extremist networks that have allowed terrorist organisations to become more prominent. )
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
495 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 173 0 R
/E (One of the most prominent organisations was Al-Qaeda, founded in 1988 by Osama Bin Laden and other militant individuals who had fought in the Afghan resistance against the soviet union. By 1998 the organisation had become prominent on an international level, and had begun to target the United States and their allies. In February 1998 the leaders of Al-Qaeda issued a statement calling for attacks on American citizens and their allies. )
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
496 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 173 0 R
/E (On the 7 th August 1998 there were two almost simultaneous truck bomb attacks on the United States embassies in Nairobi, Kenya and Dar es Salaam, Tanzania. This resulted in the deaths of 224 people including 12 Americans. Over 4,500 people were injured; the majority were Kenyan and Tanzanians. This showed how effective Al-Qaeda could be when carrying out planned attacks on an international scale. The organisation became a significant threat to international security. It raised concerns about how terrorist organisations can carry out attacks across national borders, and gain funding and support. This also showed how vulnerable the security systems of nations are. )
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
497 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 174 0 R
/Pg 29 0 R
/A [981 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
498 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (Al-Qaeda was founded in 1988 by Osama bin Laden and other militants who had fought in the Afghan resistance against the Soviets. It originated in a network of groups supporting foreign fighters in Afghanistan. However, it started to develop more aims. )
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
499 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (An international network formed during the early 1990s. Al-Qaeda would fund, provide logistical support, train and organise militant groups. Between 1991 and 1996, Osama bin Laden and a few of his associates were based in Sudan. They built up connections with funding and organisation. Bin Laden however left Sudan in 1996 due to pressure from the international community. He moved to Afghanistan which the Taliban now held much of the country. )
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
500 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (In February 1998, bin Laden and other extremist leaders released a statement from the World Islamic Front for Jihad Against Jews and Crusaders. They announced an attack on American civilians, soldiers and their allies. This shows how the organisation wanted to expand internationally. )
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
501 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (Al-Qaeda had become more involved with international terrorism by 1998. They carried out co-ordinated bombings on the American embassies in Kenya and Tanzania. )
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
502 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (power rivalry and their respective military alliances. After the fall of the Soviet Union, international focus began to move towards regional conflicts, domestic instability and non-state armed groups. )
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
503 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (There were many regions that had suffered due to weak government, civil war, economic hardship and political issues including the Middle East and Africa. This provided fertile ground for some extremist groups to develop networks, recruit new members, receive funding and cross borders. )
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
504 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (The Soviet-Afghan War \(1979-1989\) also played a key role in the development of international extremist networks. Foreign fighters travelled to Afghanistan to fight during the conflict. Once the Soviets had left some of these individuals continued fighting and formed links with extremist groups. This helped create Al-Qaeda and other transnational movements. )
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
