509 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (In 1992 the GIA began attacking the Algerian government. They became more targeted towards civilians, journalists, intellectuals and foreigners. During the mid to late 1990's they were responsible for many massacres, predominantly in rural areas. This shows how Terrorist groups can create threats to National Governments as well as Civilians and whole regions. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
510 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (In 1998 there were disagreements within the group over the increasing random violence. This led to Hassan Hattab \(one of the commanders\) splitting off and forming his own group called the Salafist Group for Preaching and Combat \(GSPC\). This shows how extremist groups can split up and how difficult it is for Governments to wipe out the groups because they are able to regroup into smaller groups. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
511 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (Egypt also saw a large amount of extremist violence during the 1990's. Two key groups were al-Gama'a al-Islamiyya \(Islamic Group\)and Egyptian Islamic Jihad. Both groups were against the Egyptian Government however they each had their own leadership. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
512 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (Al-Gama'a al-Islamiyya attacked the Government through violence. They targeted Government workers, Security Forces and Civilians. One of the most well known attacks was on the 17 th November 1997 when militants killed 62 people in Luxor. This included Foreign Tourists. This attack received international criticism and placed more pressure on the Egyptian government to increase their anti-terrorism efforts. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
513 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (During 1998 the Egyptians continued their efforts against extremist groups. Al-Gama'a al-Islamiyya was severely affected by Government activity. However there were still other militant groups active. This shows how domestic extremist groups can become international security threats. Especially when the members become connected to groups in other countries. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
