517 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (At the close of 1999, the danger posed by transnational terrorist networks continues to be significant, unpredictable, and pervasive. The terrorist attacks in East Africa have clearly demonstrated the security vulnerabilities around the world and confirmed that non-state groups have the ability to exploit weaknesses in state governments, porous borders, and local conditions such as poverty and instability to create a solid base. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
518 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (The police in the East African countries of Kenya and Tanzania, along with international law-enforcement officials, are actively investigating the local terrorist infrastructure after the attacks which have damaged regional economies and destroyed tourism potential while highlighting limitations of anti-terror capabilities in developing countries. Such governments find it very difficult to maintain border security, manage informal financial flows, and protect their citizens without large amounts of foreign funding and assistance. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
519 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (In Afghanistan, the Taliban government controls the country and shelters Osama bin Laden and the leadership of his Al-Qaeda network. Despite United Nations Security Council Resolutions 1214 and 1267, the Taliban refused to comply with the international demands for the surrender of bin Laden and closure of training camps. According to the Taliban, bin Laden is the guest of the Afghan government under its hospitality rules and demands official recognition of its government in exchange for any concessions. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
520 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (However, the world community is divided in its efforts against transnational terrorism. The unilateral cruise missile strikes by the US in Sudan and Afghanistan under Operation Infinite Reach continue to cause legal and diplomatic controversy. Countries have to choose between sovereign rights under Article 2\(4\) of the United Nations Charter and right to self-defense under Article 51 for striking a non-state actor on the territory of another state. Meanwhile, Resolution 1267 has led to an attempt at the creation of multilateral and targeted sanctions, although implementation of asset freezes and travel bans in diverse financial and legal environments is not effective and allows non-state organizations to evade the sanctions. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
521 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (An important question that the Security Council should answer in this case is what is the legal framework for military action against the non-state actors on the territory of the foreign, neutral or uncooperative state. States threatened by the transnational networks argue that )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
