525 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Transnational terrorist groups require safe havens for recruiting, training, planning, and conducting global terrorist operations. The Security Council has to address the problem of state responsibility in the cases when the host governments tolerate or even permit foreign terrorist groups on their territory. It will have to determine whether the failure or refusal of the state to dismantle terrorist infrastructure is the violation of international law and which types of enforcement, ranging from diplomatic isolation to binding Chapter VII sanctions, should be employed to force recalcitrant regimes, like the Taliban regime, to cooperate. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
526 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (These networks finance their activities through a combination of front companies, false charitable organizations, criminal organizations, and an informal unregulated money transfer system called hawala. The existing banking regulations and sanction system is often ineffective against the decentralized non-banking infrastructure of these networks. The Security Council will have to determine how to promote international financial transparency and curtail illegal financing of terrorists without undermining legitimate remittances and trade. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
527 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (As terrorist organisations begin to carry out more international activity there has been a rise )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
528 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (in increased co-operation between national intelligence and law enforcement agencies. Due to terrorist networks becoming active in multiple countries, intelligence gathered by one government can be key in stopping attacks occurring. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
529 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (However there are many issues with intelligence co-operation. Often governments are unwilling to provide any sensitive information. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
530 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Article 51 of the United Nations Charter provides inherent right of self-defense through direct strike against terrorist safe havens when the host state is unwilling or unable to stop the threat. Meanwhile, the opposing member states argue that unauthorized military strikes against another country violate sovereign rights of that country under Article 2\(4\) and undermine the power of the Security Council which was created as the unique body which should authorize all military operations. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
