/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (The Security Council should consider how international intelligence sharing can be improved whilst still keeping national security information private. Delegates should consider if the United Nations should promote a centralised intelligence database or if intelligence co-operation should remain between individual governments. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
536 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (One of the main issues with international anti-terrorism efforts is the differing definitions of terrorism. Many governments have historically defined armed organisations based on their political goals, methods and actions. Therefore an armed organisation may be seen as a terrorist group by one government but may not be seen in the same way by another. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
537 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (This causes issues when trying to create international agreements, investigations and prosecutions. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
538 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (The Security Council should consider if an international definition is needed to define terrorism. They should also consider what activities would be included within the definition. Finally they should consider how international law should differentiate between intentional attacks on civilians and armed conflict. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
539 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (With the increase in terrorist organisations, many governments have implemented increased security measures such as surveillance, incarceration, increased border security and increased police powers.Whilst these may help stop terrorist attacks, they can cause serious human rights issues. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
540 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (Many countries suffering from extremist violence have had increased arrests, military actions and limitations on civilian activities as part of their anti-terrorism campaign. The international community should therefore consider how governments can protect their citizens whilst still maintaining basic human rights. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
541 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (The Security Council should consider how important it is to have fair processes, to protect people from being incarcerated without reason and to treat civilians fairly during anti-terrorism operations. Delegates should consider if international monitoring could help ensure security measures are in line with international law. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
