593 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (- UN Security Council Resolution 1269 \(Oct 19, 1999\): Condemns all acts of terrorism unconditionally as criminal and unjustifiable and urges states to adopt international counter-terrorism conventions and cooperate on intelligence and denying safe havens. )
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
594 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (Until late 1999, attempts to solve the problem of transnational terrorism focused on unilateral measures and domestic executive orders. Various states, especially the U.S., enacted decrees for freezing assets of terrorists' leaders and criminalizing any financial transactions with them. Although these national measures prevented access to domestic financial institutions, they did not prevent money flows in international informal networks and offshore financial centers \(hawala\). Without universal and binding international mechanisms, terrorist groups were able to redirect finances through non-complying states. )
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
595 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (Recognizing the limitations of unilateral actions, the UN Security Council attempted to establish binding and multilateral sanctions through Resolution 1267 in October 1999. Through creating the Sanctions Committee and imposing sanctions in form of freezing assets and banning flights of Taliban, the UN created a modern non-military coercive mechanism under Chapter VII. However, this mechanism faced the following problem: its success totally depended on willingness of member states to comply with it and they did not possess necessary financial intelligence mechanisms or legislation for tracking informal finances and closing borders. As a result, the Security Council faced a task of development of universal enforcement mechanisms for implementation of multilateral sanctions. )
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
